WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:06.509
2017 has been one of the craziest years
in the eSports history.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:12.719
Faker is destroyed. Just this year we've had more than a dozen non-endemic investors buy

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:19.020
into eSports, the announcement and
creation of a franchised NA LCS and a

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:32.369
total shift in the Dota 2 pro system. But
we also saw the implosion of a major

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:37.950
eSports team and scandals that brought
back memories of how eSports was before

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:47.190
the scene was professionalized. 2017 was
a transitional year, it was a year

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.980
we moved further away from the grassroots origins of eSports and further into a

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.870
professionalized model designed to
attract outside money and viewers into

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:03.629
the scene but it was also a year rocked
by scandals, deals gone wrong and an

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:10.740
entire league being left out to dry.
2017 proved that eSports is growing but

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.799
it hasn't gone past growing pains just
yet.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:21.330
In January we began to see the first
signs of investment from non-endemic

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:26.450
sports organizations into eSports. The
Miami Heat entered into a strategic

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:31.380
partnership with Misfits which has so far
seen Misfits players show up at the

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.049
Heats NBA games and Misfits itself get a
spot in the Overwatch League. The Heat

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:41.390
weren't the only pro sports team to
invest into eSports this month either as

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:47.220
Milwaukee Bucks co-owner Wesley Edens
also founded Flyquest in January.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.020
Speaking of eSports teams expanding, Echo Fox signed the majority of their

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.060
all-star fighting game roster this month
including future EVO champion

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:04.500
Hajime “Tokido” Taniguchi and Mortal Kombat star Dominique “SonicFox” McLean.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.540
Finally right at the end of January Astralis won the first cs:go major of the year with a

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.040
2-1 win over Virtus.pro in Atlanta.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.160
CASTER AUDIO

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.200
And Astralis, they win the first

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:25.560
major championship
16:14 against Virtus.pro!

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.640
February was a pretty slow month but in more sports orgs are buying up eSports teams News

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:39.450
Delaware North purchased a controlling
stake in Splyce. February did however

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.489
bring the first major scandal of the
year as Tainted Minds coach

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:51.420
Nick “Inero” Smith came forward with allegations that his team was being mistreated by their organization.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.780
So Tainted Minds was a huge story this year and it was drawn

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.580
out over several months over several
reports basically it boiled down to an

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.690
oceanic League of Legends team, Tainted Minds and the way in which it was

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:09.120
treating its players. It started with Inero basically coming forward and

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.760
telling the public through a TwitLonger
that there were terrible conditions in

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.840
the team house, there were allegations
that air conditioning wasn't working and

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.120
this was summer in Australia, that there
were insufficient computers for them to

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:29.250
practice that garbage was piling up in
the house because basically there wasn't

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:35.220
enough city sponsored trash bins to
collect all the trash. There were all of

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.489
these problems and that the team was
basically locked in a contract dispute

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:42.540
the players rather, were locked in a
contract dispute with Tainted Minds. Over

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.900
the course of the next couple of months
we spoke to many of the players, we spoke

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.690
to their manager who outlined a lot of
the problems they'd had with the

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:53.100
organization. Ultimately all of this
culminates in a Riot investigation into the team.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.040
The Tainted Minds saga
continued into March but as it did the

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.280
rest of eSports kept moving too. Team
Liquid began to shuffle around their

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.260
League of Legends roster this month
bringing on

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.750
Yiliang “Doublelift” Peng to save them from relegation, putting rookie AD carry

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.940
Jung “Youngbin” Young-bin out of a job.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.080
Man that organization has had a history.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:24.060
It seems like with Steve Arhancet set, he's been a great boss. It seems

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.550
like he does really care about his players but the team

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:31.440
itself doesn't seem to know how to
manage their roster or pick players and

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.880
their teamwork isn't really good. They
seem to always want to find the quickest

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.380
solution and so they put money into
places that would get them that Band Aid

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.180
solution but then it doesn't always work
out. So this year we saw them, you know

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:49.830
they got Reignover who was freaking good
in Europe. Immortals, like he's

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.190
amazing but some reason he just fell
through it in Liquid. Does Liquid just

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.150
seem to ruin players? Piglet? I don't know.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:03.510
Also in March Optic Gaming won the 2017
Halo World Championship with a 4-0 sweep

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.740
over team EnVy.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:12.520
Casters: We are taking a look at the end of this game.This one's over!

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:25.480
Ladies and gentlemen Optic Gaming are your 2017 Halo World Champions.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:31.340
Moving on to April we saw Team SoloMid
win the NA LCS Spring Finals in

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.460
Vancouver. While Origen, the team founded by

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:40.550
legendary League of Legends midlaner Enrique “xPeke” Cedeño Martinez,

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:47.150
was relegated from the EU LCS after split
after split of poor performances Origen

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:53.120
Casters: Origen gets 3-0. Origen's nightmare come to an end as Giants keep their LCS dream alive.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.560
But the most important thing that
happened in April was that theScore

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:03.050
esports broke the news that Riot Games
was franchising the NA LCS, which had

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.140
wide reaching implications across all of
eSports for the rest of the year and

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:09.980
definitely beyond that.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:15.540
I think NA LCS franchising is probably still the

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.900
biggest story of the year for me at
least. Basically with a move towards a

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:24.289
franchised model Riot is trying to
duplicate with League of Legends what

00:06:24.289 --> 00:06:29.600
traditional sports leagues have done and
they're trying to create basically an

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.430
ecosystem in North America where the
investors, the sponsors, the teams, all

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.050
feel comfortable and feel like they can
experiment. Basically going back even

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:44.660
before this year, we heard obviously from
basically every LCS team owner that they

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:50.270
were unable to turn a profit with their
teams that because of the way that the

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.919
NA and EU LCS were set up. They were
losing money it wasn't a successful

00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:58.520
venture and that this was scaring away
sponsors. Sponsors didn't want to

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.680
invest in teams that, A were unable
to turn a profit, but B also the fear of

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:08.900
relegation. With a franchise system, teams
can bring in players and not be

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:12.470
afraid. They can take some time to
develop talent and rookies and it

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:17.570
basically just gives everyone a safety
cushion to really focus on being the

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.480
best teams that they can, rather than
just staying out of relegation

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:22.860
guaranteeing that performance split to
split.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:30.160
Also this month, the International 2016 champions, Wings Gaming left their organization following

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.300
reports that players weren't paid on
time and that the organization took the

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:39.439
majority of their prize money. The
players formed Team Random but collapsed

00:07:39.439 --> 00:07:43.999
in May after a last place finish at the
Kiev major and have since scattered

00:07:43.999 --> 00:07:48.199
across several Chinese teams.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:58.320
CASTER AUDIO

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:03.880
Wings are your International 2016 champions!

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:15.500
Terrific display of Dota.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:27.780
Nothing else happened in May, so let's skip that in favor of something more exciting.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.820
June kicked off with Riot Games officially announcing

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:39.220
that NA LCS franchising was happening
detailing the application process and

00:08:39.229 --> 00:08:45.080
revealing that they would kickstart a
players association to advocate for NA

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:50.839
LCS pros. Also this month, we saw a lot of
colleagues lose their jobs when Yahoo

00:08:50.839 --> 00:08:56.480
Esports shut down following Verizon's
acquisition of Yahoo. Several former

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.790
Yahoo Esports writers and staff found
new homes elsewhere but unfortunately it

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:08.980
wasn't the last time an eSports news
site shut down this year.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.980
Moving on to July, League of Legends franchising saw its first result as the Madison Square

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:22.130
Garden Company bought CLG, adding yet
another sports org to the LCS. Then just

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.180
ahead of the International, Valve
released their plan to change the Dota

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:30.880
pro circuit adding several more Majors
and Minors in a year.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:36.080
Valve's rework of the major/minor system is a new system by which teams will be determined to play

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.580
at the International but also support
third party tournament organizers in the

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:46.250
Dota scene. So any tournament that has a
minimum required prize pool will be

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:50.330
either assigned as a Major or a Minor by
Valve and have an additional prize pool

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:53.180
equivalent to that supplemented to the
tournaments.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.920
It's a big deal because in a lot of ways it kind of gives back a lot of power to the tournament

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.380
organizers this is something that
they've been talking about a lot of

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:08.450
majors though the majors have been
organized by third party companies there

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:15.170
were only so many of them. But now it's a
new major/minor system. Many

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:21.800
more are representing Valve and Dota
competitions. New companies are

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:23.440
representing it as well.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:30.380
In tournament wins this month, Gambit won the major with a shock 2-1 victory over Immortals propelled by

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.320
the best grenade throw in major history.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:41.580
Casters: This is huge.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:55.100
Also this month, we saw Hajime “Tokido” Taniguchi, Adam “Armada” Lindgren and

00:10:55.100 --> 00:11:03.600
Saleem “Salem” Akkiel-Young crowned EVO champions in EVO 2017’s three biggest tournaments.

